.TH VA2000 7 "15 November 2026" "va2000"
.SH NAME
va2000 \- the MNT VA2000 RTG graphics card
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /dev/va2000
.br
.B #include \(dqva2000.h\(dq
.SH DESCRIPTION
The MNT VA2000 is a graphics card for the Zorro bus that also passes the
Amiga's own picture through to its monitor output.
.B /dev/va2000
gives programs its registers and its framebuffer: they set a display mode,
draw into the framebuffer through
.BR mmap (2),
and switch the monitor output between the Amiga's picture and the card's.
The X server
.B Xrtg
is such a program.
.PP
The card is found by AutoConfig, manufacturer 0x6D6E, product 0x01, with
firmware version 5 or later.
Its address space is the same in both bus modes:
.TP 20
.B 0x000000\-0x000FFF
the registers, 16 bits wide;
.TP
.B 0x010000\-
the framebuffer, to the end of the aperture.
.PP
The aperture is 4 MB on a Zorro II board and 32 MB on a Zorro III board; the
driver takes its size from AutoConfig.
Minor 0 is the first board, minor 1 the second.
.PP
The driver is linked into the kernel, or is the loadable kernel module
.BR va2000 ,
of the demand class: the module core loads it at the first open of
.BR /dev/va2000 ,
and keeps it only when a board answers.
The module cannot be unloaded.
.SS The picture
Opening the device changes nothing on the screen.
A program shows the card's picture by setting a mode, through
.B SVGAIOCSetScreenMode
or by writing the mode registers through its own mapping.
At the last close of the device the driver puts the Amiga's picture back
(passthrough), so a program that dies does not leave the monitor on the card.
.SH IOCTLS
The argument is a pointer for the requests that pass a structure or a value
in memory; the others return their result as the value of
.BR ioctl (2).
.TP
.B VA2IOC_GETFW
the firmware version.
.TP
.BR VA2IOC_GETSCREENW ", " VA2IOC_GETSCREENH
the width and height of the last mode set with
.BR SVGAIOCSetScreenMode ,
0 before it.
.TP
.B VA2IOC_GETCOLORMODE
the value read from the card's colour mode register (0 is 8 bits per pixel,
1 is 16).
The card's registers have read back the firmware version on the firmware
tried so far, so this answer is not reliable.
.PP
The
.B SVGAIOC
requests follow the
.B svgaioctl.h
interface of the Amix SVGA X servers:
.TP
.B SVGAIOCGetBoardData
fills a
.BR "struct SVGABoardData" :
card id 16, the framebuffer size, a pixel clock limit of 100 MHz, blitter
and panning present.
.TP
.B SVGAIOCGetFBufSize
the framebuffer size in bytes, as the value of the call.
.TP
.B SVGAIOCSetScreenMode
sets a mode from a
.BR "struct SVGAScreenMode" .
The visible size is
.B SVGASM_HDisplayEnd
by
.BR SVGASM_VDisplayEnd ,
not the width and height fields.
.B SVGASM_ColorMode
is
.B SVGACM_8BIT
or 16 bits per pixel for any other value.
A mode of at most 640 by 360 is shown doubled in both directions.
The pixel clock is rounded up to 40, 75 or 100 MHz.
.TP
.B SVGAIOCGetScreenData
fills a
.B struct SVGAScreenData
for the last mode set with
.BR SVGAIOCSetScreenMode .
.TP
.BR SVGAIOCGetMonitorSwitch ", " SVGAIOCSetMonitorSwitch
an unsigned short:
.B SVGAMONITORSWITCH_Amiga
(1) puts the Amiga's picture back;
.B SVGAMONITORSWITCH_SVGA
(2) turns passthrough off, but does not restore the card's mode: the program
sets its mode again after it.
Another value gives
.BR EINVAL .
.TP
.B SVGAIOCBlankScreen
an unsigned short: 0 shows the Amiga's picture, another value the card's.
.TP
.B SVGAIOCClearBoardMem
clears the visible part of the framebuffer with the blitter (16-bit modes).
.TP
.B SVGAIOCSetRGB
four unsigned longs, the index and the red, green and blue values: sets one
entry of the 256-entry palette of the 8-bit mode.
.TP
.BR SVGAIOCGetRGB ", " SVGAIOCGetPanPos ", " SVGAIOCGetBorderColor
answer with fixed values: the palette cannot be read back, and panning and
the border colour are not implemented.
.TP
.BR SVGAIOCSetPanPos ", " SVGAIOCSetPanXPos ", " SVGAIOCSetPanYPos ", " SVGAIOCSetBorderColor ", " SVGAIOCStartBlit ", " SVGAIOCEndBlit ", " SVGAIOCDisableScrGrp ", " SVGAIOCEnableScrGrp
accepted and do nothing.
.SH MMAP
.BR mmap (2)
of the device maps the card's address space from offset 0 to the end of the
aperture: the registers at offset 0, the framebuffer at offset 0x10000.
The pages are the card's own; the mapping lasts while the program holds it.
.SH READ AND WRITE
.BR read (2)
and
.BR write (2)
reach the same address space at the file offset.
They are slow and exist for compatibility; programs use
.BR mmap (2).
.SH SUPPORTED BOARDS AND KERNELS
.TP 4
\(bu
a 68030 kernel, Zorro II board: the registers are reached at the board's
AutoConfig address;
.TP
\(bu
a 68030 kernel, Zorro III board (at 0x40000000 or above): the registers are
mapped into the kernel, and so is a temporary window for each
.B read
and
.BR write .
The module does this; the static form only when built with
.BR VA2000_SPT ;
.TP
\(bu
a 68040 or 68060 kernel, either bus: the registers are mapped noncacheable
and serialised.
In the static form the framebuffer is also given the faster noncacheable,
not serialised class; the module leaves it at the kernel's default class.
.SH UNTESTED
The card cannot be emulated.
The static form has run on a 68040 and a 68060 with a Zorro III board, and
on a 68030 with a Zorro II board before the Zorro III changes.
The module has not run on hardware on either kernel line, and the 68030
Zorro III path has never run.
Byte-wide register access has not been tried; the driver makes only aligned
16-bit accesses.
.SH ERRORS
.TP 10
.B ENXIO
no board for this minor, the board does not answer, or a
.BR read " or " write
offset beyond the aperture.
.TP
.B EINVAL
an unknown request, or a monitor switch value other than 1 or 2.
.TP
.B EFAULT
a bad argument pointer.
.TP
.B ENOMEM
.BR read " or " write
could not map its window.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/va2000
character major 68, minor 0.
.TP
.B /etc/conf/mod.d/va2000
the module.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ioctl (2),
.BR mmap (2)
